What this means if you are just using the app
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Nothing changes for you. Download it, install it, clip your streams, post the
clips, make money from them. The AGPL places obligations on people who
DISTRIBUTE the software or run a MODIFIED version as a network service — not
on people who use it. Your videos and your clips are yours; the licence has
nothing to say about them.

If you modify Clips Studio and give that modified version to someone else, or
run it as a service other people use over a network, you have to offer them
the source of your modified version under this same licence.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why a copyleft licence
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

It keeps improvements to the tool available to the creators it was written
for, rather than allowing a closed fork of the same work to be sold back to
them.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Windows installer bundles the Python engine and its dependencies. The
significant ones, with their licences:

  ultralytics (YOLOv8)      AGPL-3.0     pose/face tracking + model weights
  TalkNet-ASD               MIT          active speaker detection; the model
                                         code is vendored unmodified under
                                         third_party/talknet/ (c) 2021 Tao
                                         Ruijie, and its pretrained weights
                                         are fetched by scripts/fetch_asd_model.py
  faster-whisper            MIT          transcription
  PyTorch                   BSD-3-Clause tensor runtime, CUDA libraries
  OpenCV (opencv-python)    Apache-2.0   frame decoding and analysis
  yt-dlp                    Unlicense    downloading source videos
  FFmpeg                    see below    all encoding, decoding and muxing
  FastAPI / Uvicorn         MIT          the local API the desktop app talks to
  Electron                  MIT          the desktop shell
  React                     MIT          the interface

FFmpeg is fetched at build time by scripts/fetch_ffmpeg.py rather than kept
in this repository. The builds used are LGPL-2.1-or-later, or GPL-2.0-or-later
where they include GPL-licensed components; the exact terms ship with the
binary in the installer.

Ollama and the language models it runs are NOT bundled. They are installed
separately by the user and carry their own licences — Gemma, for example, is
covered by the Gemma Terms of Use rather than by anything here.


